WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.690
The shoreline is not necessarily the edge of continental crust.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.650
A continental shelf extends underwater with a gentle slope.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:12.750
The continental slope descends toward the deep ocean, and a continental

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.730
rise can form a sediment apron farther out.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.470
Submarine canyons cut parts of the shelf and slope.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.950
Passive margins often have broad shelves, while active margins commonly have

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:27.890
narrower ones; shelf width alone is not a boundary diagnosis.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.710
In the source cross section, normal faults thin continental crust toward

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.910
the ocean. Sediment buries the downdropped blocks, and the crust changes

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.430
from continental material toward thinner basaltic and gabbroic oceanic crust.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.490
A passive margin preserves this older extension even when no active

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.430
plate boundary lies along the coast.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:52.270
The sedimentary cover and buried structures can also create petroleum-bearing settings.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:58.370
Weak, unconsolidated slope sediment can fail after shaking, storms or overloading.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.990
Mixing with seawater can create a dense turbidity current that moves

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:03.590
downhill and erodes a canyon.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.530
Some canyon heads also inherited river erosion from lower sea levels.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:11.210
A submarine landslide can instead move comparatively coherent blocks.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.790
The current, the landslide and the canyon are related but different

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:15.190
things.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.270
When a current leaves a steep canyon, it spreads and slows.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:24.070
Larger grains settle first, followed by finer silt and clay, producing

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:27.050
a graded bed with a coarse base and a finer top.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.850
Repeated events stack many graded beds.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.610
The spreading deposit builds a submarine fan at the slope foot

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.910
and across the rise. Lecture Fifteen emphasizes the link between sediment

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:40.770
source, transport pathway and depositional body.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:46.330
Use the original numbered figures to distinguish shelf, slope, canyon, current,

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.970
fan, graded bed and landslide.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.230
A fan is a deposit, while a canyon is an erosional

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.670
pathway; graded bedding is a pattern inside a bed.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:59.070
The Bengal Fan illustrates the scale these systems can reach.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:03.530
The essential explanation connects where sediment starts, how it moves, and

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.970
what remains after the flow loses energy.
